==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|ër}}, from {{inh|de|goh|er}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*iʀ}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*iz}}. In northern {{cog|gmh|-}} and {{cog|goh|-}} there also existed forms with initial ''h-'', namely {{m+|gmh|her}}, {{m+|goh|her}}, from {{m+|gem-pro|*hiz}}, whence {{cog|gmw-cfr|hä}} and (from the accusative) {{cog|lb|hien}}. Compare {{m+|en|he}}. The unusual spelling ''ih-'' in the forms {{m|de|ihm}}, {{m|de|ihn}} is not related to this. It was introduced in early modern German to distinguish these forms from {{m|de|im}}, {{m|de|in}} (when {{m|de||*iem}}, {{m|de||*ien}} could have been read as {{m|de||*jem}}, {{m|de||*jen}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{qualifier|standard}} {{IPA|de|/eːr/|[ʔeːɐ̯]|[ʔɛɐ̯]}}
:* {{audio|de|De-er.ogg}}
:* {{audio|de|De-er2.ogg}}
:* {{audio|de|De-er-1.ogg}}
:* {{rhymes|de|eːɐ̯|s=1}}
:* {{homophones|de|Ehr}}
* {{qualifier|colloquially in unstressed position}} {{IPA|de|/ɐ/}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|de|pronoun|cat2=personal pronouns}}

# {{lb|de|personal}} [[he]].
#: {{uxi|de|Wo ist Klaus? Wo ist '''er'''?|t=Where is Klaus? Where is '''he'''?|audio=De-wo ist klaus.ogg}}
#: {{uxi|de|Dies ist mein Hund. '''Er''' heißt Waldi.|t=This is my dog. '''His''' name is Waldi.|audio=De-Dies ist mein hund.ogg}}
# {{lb|de|personal}} [[it]] (when the grammatical gender of the object/article/thing/animal etc., being referred to, is masculine ({{m|de|der}})).
#: {{uxi|de|Dort steht ein Baum. '''Er''' ist über hundert Jahre alt.|t=There stands a tree. '''It''' is more than 100 years old.|audio=De-Dort steht ein baum.ogg}}
# {{lb|de|personal}} [[she]] (when the grammatical gender of the noun being referred to and designating a female person, is masculine ({{m|de|der}})).
#: {{uxi|de|Im Frauengefängnis versuchte ein Häftling zu flüchten, aber '''er''' kam nicht weit.|t=In the women’s prison, an inmate tried to escape, but '''she''' didn’t get very far.|audio=De-Im Frauengefängnis versuchte ein Häftling zu flüchten, aber er kam nicht weit..ogg}}
# {{lb|de|personal|archaic}} {{alt sp|de|Er|tr=you (polite)}}
#* {{rfdate|de}}, {{w|Clemens Brentano}}, ''Geschichte vom braven Kasperl und dem schönen Annerl'' (edited). In: '''1835''', F. W. Gubitz (editor), ''Jahrbuch des Nützlichen und Unterhaltenden für 1835'', p. 171:
#*: {{quote|de|Da fuhr die Alte überraſcht auf und ſprach: Lieber Herr, gehe '''er''' doch nach Haus und bete '''er''' fein und lege '''er''' ſich ſchlafen.| translation=Then the old woman sprang up, surprised, and said: Dear gentleman, do go home and say your prayers and go to bed.}}
#* {{quote-book|de|year=1837|author=w:Brothers Grimm| title=Kinder- und Haus-Märchen, Band 2| chapter=Der junge Riese| url=https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Der_junge_Riese_(1837)| page=27| text=Da sprach er „Vater, ich sehe wohl, bei '''ihm''' werd ich nicht satt, will '''er''' mir einen Stab von Eisen verschaffen, der stark ist, und den ich vor meinen Knien nicht zerbrechen kann, so will ich wieder fort gehen.“ Da war der Bauer froh, und spannte seine zwei Pferde vor den Wagen, fuhr zum Schmied, und holte einen Stab so groß und dick, als ihn die zwei Pferde nur fahren konnten.| translation=Then he said: "Father, I can see that I shall not be able to eat my fill here. If '''you''' bring me a strong rod of iron that I cannot break, I shall go away again." Then the farmer was glad, and he harnessed his horses to the wagon, drove to the smithy, and fetched a rod so long and thick that his two horses could barely pull it.}}

====Declension====
{{de-decl-personal pronouns}}

* In contemporary German, the genitive forms of personal pronouns are restricted to formal style and are infrequent even then. They may be used:
** for the genitive object still found in a handful of verbs: ''Ich erbarmte mich '''seiner'''.'' – "I had mercy on him". (Colloquially one would either use the dative case, or a prepositional object, or replace the verb with another.)
** with certain adjectives or prepositions that govern the genitive, such as {{m|de|statt}} ("instead of, in place of"): ''Ich kam statt '''seiner''' in die Mannschaft.'' – I joined the team in his place. (This sounds antiquated, for which reason ''an seiner Statt'' or ''an seiner Stelle'' is preferable.)
* Older forms/spellings include:
** {{m|de|jm}} {{q|dative; 16th century}}, {{m|de|jn}} {{q|accusative; 16th century}} – distinguished from {{m|de|im||in the, into the}} and {{m|de|in||in, into}}
** {{m|de|ihme}} {{q|dative}}

